## Streaming Large Uploads in ASP.NET Core 8 Minimal API

`IFormFile` buffers the entire request body in memory by default. For large files, read directly from the request body stream and write to disk in chunks.

### Key changes needed:

**1. Disable request size limits and form buffering:**

```csharp
// Program.cs
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Raise the max request body size (default is ~28MB)
builder.WebHost.ConfigureKestrel(options =>
{
    options.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024; // 2 GB
});

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapPost("/upload", async (HttpRequest request) =>
{
    // Ensure the request has content
    if (!request.HasFormContentType)
    {
        return Results.BadRequest("Expected a multipart/form-data request.");
    }

    var boundary = request.GetMultipartBoundary();
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(boundary))
    {
        return Results.BadRequest("Missing multipart boundary.");
    }

    var reader = new Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities.MultipartReader(boundary, request.Body);
    var section = await reader.ReadNextSectionAsync();

    while (section != null)
    {
        var hasContentDisposition = Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers
            .ContentDispositionHeaderValue
            .TryParse(section.ContentDisposition, out var contentDisposition);

        if (hasContentDisposition && contentDisposition!.DispositionType == "form-data"
            && !string.IsNullOrEmpty(contentDisposition.FileName.Value))
        {
            // Stream directly to disk — never holds the full file in memory
            var trustedFileName = Path.GetRandomFileName();
            var savePath = Path.Combine("/tmp/uploads", trustedFileName);

            await using var targetStream = File.Create(savePath);
            await section.Body.CopyToAsync(targetStream);

            return Results.Ok(new { savedAs = trustedFileName });
        }

        section = await reader.ReadNextSectionAsync();
    }

    return Results.BadRequest("No file found in request.");
})
.DisableAntiforgery();

app.Run();
```

**2. Disable form value count/size limits (optional but recommended):**

```csharp
builder.Services.Configure<Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Features.FormOptions>(options =>
{
    options.MultipartBodyLengthLimit = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024;
    options.BufferBody = false; // critical — don't buffer
});
```

### Why this works

| Approach | Memory usage for 2 GB file |
|----------|---------------------------|
| `IFormFile` | ~2 GB (buffered) |
| `MultipartReader` | ~80 KB (streaming) |

`MultipartReader` reads the multipart boundaries incrementally and exposes each section as a forward-only stream. `CopyToAsync` copies in 80 KB chunks by default.

### If using reverse proxy (Nginx/IIS)

Also raise limits there:

```nginx
# nginx
client_max_body_size 2048m;
```

```xml
<!-- web.config for IIS -->
<system.webServer>
  <security>
    <requestFiltering>
      <requestLimits maxAllowedContentLength="2147483648" />
    </requestFiltering>
  </security>
</system.webServer>
```

### Tips

- **Never trust the client filename** — use `Path.GetRandomFileName()` or a GUID.
- Add a `CancellationToken` to `CopyToAsync` for client disconnect handling.
- For progress tracking, wrap the stream copy in a loop reading fixed-size buffers manually.
